186351165 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351165 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ be a basis of ${R}^{2}$ and $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ What are the coordinates of $x$ relative to the basis $C=\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\},$ where ${c}_{1}=3{b}_{2}$ and ${c}_{2}={b}_{1}+6{b}_{2}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351165 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2eedfab1-a2e1-5bd8-81d2-7c293aaffcd9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351165 #edb/ref 1074 "We are given that\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}=3{b}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{2}={b}_{1}+6{b}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we express the $b$'s in terms of $c$'s. From the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n{b}_{2}=\\frac{1}{3}{c}_{1}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{c}_{2}={b}_{1}+6(\\frac{1}{3}{c}_{1})\\,⟹\\,{b}_{1}={c}_{2}-2{c}_{1}.\n$$\n\nSince $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$, we can write the vector $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-5{b}_{1}+2{b}_{2} \\\\  & =-5({c}_{2}-2{c}_{1})+2(\\frac{1}{3}{c}_{1}) \\\\  & =-5{c}_{2}+10{c}_{1}+\\frac{2}{3}{c}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{3}{c}_{1}-5{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{32}{3} \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186355221 #edb/ref 1080 "y=A{e}^{2x}+B{e}^{-3x}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186355221 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186355221 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "3e1ea764-4dca-5820-9034-d4b4f70f730b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186355466 #edb/ref 1080 "y=A{e}^{2x}+B{e}^{3x}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186355466 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186355466 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "5ae4ce25-9e97-5331-8ed8-96b2d9024430" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186355900 #edb/ref 1080 "y=A{e}^{x}+B{e}^{6x}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186355900 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186355900 #edb/ref 1083 #